sangati chala-apeksha

  -- chala-apeksha: a moving reference frame — the denominator itself is in motion
  --
  -- the canonical case in robotics:
  --   joint i's local frame is the apeksha for joint i+1's position
  --   but joint i's frame moves whenever joint i (or any ancestor joint) changes angle
  --   so the apeksha is itself rotating and translating in world-space
  --
  -- kramanusara wrt chala-apeksha = total derivative (includes transport term):
  --
  --   (d/dt)_world = (d/dt)_local + ω × (·)
  --
  --   where ω is the angular velocity of the moving frame itself
  --   this extra term is what produces:
  --     Coriolis acceleration:   2 · ω × v_local
  --     centripetal acceleration: ω × (ω × r)
  --
  -- in a kinematic chain: every joint except the base has a chala-apeksha
  -- the base joint has sthira-apeksha (fixed to the world)
  --
  -- for torque computation: ignoring chala-apeksha (using τ = I·α alone)
  -- is only valid when joints are slow or independent. At speed the
  -- coupling terms between joints become significant.
  --
  -- the correct equation (Newton-Euler, recursive):
  --   τ[i] = I[i]·α[i] + ω[i] × (I[i]·ω[i])   ← chala-apeksha term
  --   plus contributions from all child links (propagated backward from tip)

  "apeksha-swarupa"
  "krama-sthita kramanusara-yukta"

  -- the frame itself has velocity and acceleration — it is a joint in the chain
  "angular-velocity-yukta angular-acceleration-yukta"
  "kona-yukta"

  -- the transport theorem: what connects local-derivative to world-derivative
  -- (d/dt)_world = (d/dt)_local + ω × (·)
  "sambandha-yukta"
  "sthira-apeksha-pratipaksha"

  -- downstream joints are chala-apeksha for their children
  -- upstream (base) joint is sthira-apeksha
  "parampara-sthita"

shabda chala-apeksha, moving-frame, body-frame, local-frame / the-reference-that-itself-moves-total-derivative-includes-transport-term-omega-cross

done
